1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Welcome back. In this video, we'll configure

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,000
VentureX's environment file.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:12,000
This controls your database connections, AI provider,

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,000
mail settings, and third-party integrations.

5
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:17,000
Let's get started.

6
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:20,000
First, copy the example environment file.

7
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:24,000
VentureX ships with .env.example containing

8
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:27,000
every supported variable with sensible defaults.

9
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:31,000
Run copy .env.example .env on Windows,

10
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:34,000
or cp .env.example .env on macOS and Linux.

11
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:38,000
File explorer confirms .env file now exists

12
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:42,000
alongside .env.example.

13
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Every Laravel application requires an encrypted

14
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:49,000
application key.

15
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:53,000
This key is used for hashing, encryption,

16
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:56,000
and secure cookie signing.

17
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Generate it now with php artisan key:generate.

18
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,000
You should see a success message confirming

19
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:08,000
the key was set.

20
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Let's walk through each section.

21
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Under APP, APP_NAME is your display name —

22
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:19,000
set it to your company name.

23
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:23,000
APP_ENV should stay local during development

24
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:26,000
and switch to production on your live server.

25
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:30,000
APP_URL is your application base URL.

26
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:34,000
For local development, keep it at localhost:8000.

27
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:38,000
APP_DEBUG should be true locally for detailed error pages,

28
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:42,000
and false in production to hide stack traces from users.

29
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:46,000
Next, the database section.

30
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:50,000
VentureX defaults to MySQL 8.

31
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Set your host to 127.0.0.1, port to 3306,

32
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:58,000
and the database name to VENTUREX_ERP — that's the default.

33
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:02,000
Enter your MySQL username and password.

34
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:06,000
If you're using the bundled XAMPP or Laragon setup,

35
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:10,000
your credentials are likely root with an empty password.

36
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:14,000
For production, use a dedicated database user

37
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:17,000
with restricted permissions.

38
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:21,000
Session settings control how user login state is managed.

39
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:25,000
SESSION_DRIVER=database stores sessions in your database

40
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:28,000
for reliability.

41
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:32,000
You can switch to redis for higher performance.

42
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:36,000
SESSION_LIFETIME is in minutes — 120 means

43
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,000
users stay logged in for two hours of inactivity.

44
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:44,000
For mail, VentureX uses smtp by default.

45
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:48,000
Configure your SMTP host, port, username,

46
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:51,000
and password for your email provider.

47
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:55,000
Set MAIL_ENCRYPTION to tls for most providers

48
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:58,000
like Gmail or SendGrid.

49
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:02,000
Now the AI Gateway — one of VentureX's standout features.

50
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:06,000
The AI_PROVIDER variable determines which AI backend

51
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:10,000
powers your insights, predictions, and chatbot.

52
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:14,000
You have four options: swift for SwiftChat AI,

53
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:17,000
gemini for Google Gemini,

54
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:21,000
deepseek for DeepSeek, and nvidia for NVIDIA's NIM platform.

55
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:25,000
NVIDIA offers a free tier at build.nvidia.com —

56
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:29,000
sign up, grab your API key, and paste it into NVIDIA_API_KEY.

57
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:33,000
For this tutorial, we'll use the NVIDIA provider

58
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:37,000
since the free tier is generous enough for development.

59
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:41,000
Finally, the optional integrations.

60
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:45,000
VentureX supports PayPal for payment processing —

61
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:49,000
just add your PAYPAL_CLIENT_ID and PAYPAL_CLIENT_SECRET.

62
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:53,000
Firebase handles push notifications and real-time features

63
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:56,000
with your FIREBASE_CREDENTIALS JSON.

64
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:00,000
Google OAuth and Microsoft OAuth enable social login —

65
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:04,000
register your app in each provider's console

66
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:08,000
and paste your client IDs and secrets here.

67
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:12,000
All of these are optional. You can run VentureX fully

68
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:15,000
without any of them configured.

69
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:19,000
If you need a deeper walkthrough on database creation

70
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:23,000
and user privileges, check the DATABASE-SETUP.md file

71
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:27,000
included in your project root.

72
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:31,000
It covers MySQL setup for both Windows and macOS

73
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:35,000
with step-by-step commands.

74
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:39,000
Your environment is configured.

75
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:43,000
In the next video, we'll install dependencies

76
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:46,000
and build the project.

77
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:50,000
See you there.
